\description{
Finds features whose abundance differs between groups without assuming
that the samples' totals mean anything (Mandal et al. 2015). Every log
ratio between two features is tested for a difference between the
groups; a feature whose abundance really changed makes most of its log
ratios differ. Each feature's \eqn{W} is the number of its log ratios that
differ significantly, and the features with a large \eqn{W} are declared
differentially abundant.
}
\details{
With \eqn{L = \log(x + c)} (natural logs, \eqn{c} the pseudocount) and
\eqn{D} features:
\enumerate{
\item For every pair of features \eqn{i < j}, the log ratio \eqn{L_i - L_j}
of each sample is tested across the groups with \code{sig_test}, giving
\eqn{p_{ij} = p_{ji}}.
\item Each feature's \eqn{D - 1} p-values are adjusted for multiple testing
as one family (\code{p_adjust}).
\item \eqn{W_i} is the number of features \eqn{j} with adjusted
\eqn{p_{ij} < \alpha}.
\item If the largest \eqn{W}, as a proportion of \eqn{D}, is below \code{theta},
nothing is rejected. Otherwise, with \eqn{c_0 = \max(W)/D}, five
cutoffs \eqn{c_0 - 0.05, \ldots, c_0 - 0.25} are tried: the
proportions of features with \eqn{W > D \cdot \mathrm{cutoff}}, and the
changes between neighbouring proportions, decide which cutoff
\eqn{\nu} is stable (the first change below \code{tau} picks \eqn{c_0 -
   0.10}, and so on, as in scikit-bio). Features with \eqn{W \ge \nu D}
are rejected.
}

\strong{Matching scikit-bio.} The results (\eqn{W} and the decisions) are
those of scikit-bio's \code{ancom()} (0.7.4), whose rules are followed
exactly, including two of its quirks:
\itemize{
\item Each feature's family of p-values for the adjustment includes a 0 for
the feature against itself. For Holm this changes nothing; for
Bonferroni, BH and BY it makes the adjustment slightly more stringent
(\eqn{D} tests instead of \eqn{D - 1}). In R terms, feature \eqn{i}'s
adjusted p-values are \code{p.adjust(c(0, p[i, -i]), p_adjust)[-1]}.
\item Its cutoffs are \eqn{c_0 - } \code{numpy.linspace(0.05, 0.25, 5)}, whose
third value is one rounding step above \code{0.15}.
}

A log ratio that is constant within every group but differs between
groups has \eqn{F = \infty} and \eqn{p = 0} with \code{sig_test = 'anova'}, as
with SciPy's \code{f_oneway()}; one that is the same in every sample has no
p-value (\code{NaN}, as in SciPy), and is left out of the feature's family and
never counted in \eqn{W}. The Kruskal-Wallis test uses average ranks for
ties and SciPy's tie correction, with ties by exact equality, as SciPy's
\code{kruskal()}; a log ratio with every value tied has no p-value (SciPy
sometimes returns an infinite statistic there, by rounding, and so a
p-value of 0). The F and \eqn{\chi^2} p-values are R's \code{\link[stats:Fdist]{stats::pf()}} and
\code{\link[stats:Chisquare]{stats::pchisq()}}, which agree with SciPy's to about \code{1e-13}. The F
statistics come from two-pass sums of squares; SciPy's one-pass ones
differ from them by up to about \code{1e-12} (relative), from rounding.

