\section{Zeros}{

Logs of zero are undefined, so zeros need replacing first. With
\code{pseudocount = NULL} (the default), no pseudocount is used when
\code{counts} has no zeros; otherwise half the smallest non-zero value is
added to every value, with a warning. The recommended input is a table
with its zeros replaced by \code{\link[=replace_zeros]{replace_zeros()}}, which has none and is
used as it is. (scikit-bio's \code{ancom()} refuses zeros; the original
ANCOM paper added a pseudocount of 0.001.)
}

\section{Same names in other packages}{

scikit-bio (Python) has \code{ancom()}, which this matches. R's ANCOMBC
package has \code{ancom()} too, an implementation of ANCOM-II with its own
zero handling and defaults; it is not what this function reproduces.
}

\section{Input Types}{


The \code{counts} parameter is designed to accept a simple numeric matrix, but
seamlessly supports objects from the following biological data packages:
\itemize{
\item \code{phyloseq}
\item \code{rbiom}
\item \code{SummarizedExperiment}
\item \code{TreeSummarizedExperiment}
}

For large datasets, standard matrix operations may be slow. See
\code{vignette('performance')} for details on using optimized formats
(e.g. sparse matrices) and parallel processing.
}

